151:
The duties and responsibilities of the commission shall be as follows:
A. To oversee the operation of all organized athletic events which take place on facilities owned by the city, with said athletic events including, but not being limited to, the following: youth baseball, youth football, youth soccer, youth softball, youth tennis and adult tennis;
B. To oversee the operation of all concessions and special events which take place on facilities owned by the city;
C. To adopt rules for governing organized league play and nonleague play ("free play");
D. To adopt rules and standards for the transaction of its business. The commission shall have the authority to adopt rules and standards without amendment to this chapter. The commission shall provide for the time and place of regular meetings and a method for the calling of special meetings. The commission shall appoint from among themselves a chairman, a vice-chairman, a secretary, and other such officers as deemed necessary for the conduct of business. A quorum shall consist of a majority of the commission's members;
E. To establish rules of conduct for patrons of the city's parks, including ethical standards and rules of good sportsmanship for team coaches, managers, players umpires and fans;
F. To establish rules governing the usage of concession facilities at the city's parks;
G. To establish park maintenance rules and regulations;
H. To establish penalties for violations of park rules;
I. To review applications for the issuance of annual league sports licenses, concessionaire licenses, and special event licenses, and to grant or deny same in accordance with the provisions of this chapter and any bylaws that the commission may promulgate;
J. To hear and resolve disputes arising from the activities at the city's parks;
K. To submit a written report of the commission's activities to the city council at least once per year;
L. To collect and to promptly submit to the city all license fees and application fees collected each year;
M. To seek and receive donations, grants and other funds on behalf of the city council for improvements to the city's parks; provided, however, that the commission shall not obligate the city without the prior consent of the city council.
(Ord. of 10-18-1993, § 5)

164:
A. One of the duties of the Committee is to prepare a master plan for the beautification of the City designed to meet present and future needs of the City. The master plan shall address the appearance and integration of common themes for all City owned facilities, public rights-of-way and easements. The master plan shall include, but is not limited to, the following:
1. A graphic presentation showing a general outline of the nature and extent of the proposed beautification of the City;
2. A suggested time schedule for the beautification, setting forth priority of projects as recommended by the Committee;
3. An estimate of the cost of the work recommended broken down into various projects and divisions of projects;
4. An estimate of the maintenance cost for the beautification of the City; and
5. A narrative discussion of the proposal, clearly explaining the need, function, and value of the master plan.
B. Other duties of the Committee shall consist of reporting to the city council from time to time on improvements of maintenance of public areas, condition of the environment throughout the City in regard to air and water pollution, and how to improve control and elimination of same. The Committee shall further render reports to the city council through the mayor on related kindred subjects as shall be requested by the city council.
(Ord. No. 98-04-13C, 4-13-1998)

